"A secret distribution diagram traces how authenticated workloads obtain, refresh, and revoke narrowly scoped credentials.\n\nUse when: Review bootstrap identity and credential delivery boundaries. Explain lease renewal, rotation, and consumer access.\n\nAvoid when: Only public certificate relationships need display.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish identity providers, secret authorities, delivery agents, and consumers. Arrows name authentication, retrieval, renewal, and revocation behaviors. Annotations identify secret classes and scopes without containing secret values.\n\nBuild: Identify the workload identity used before any secret retrieval. Draw the authority and the supported delivery mechanism. Add lease, refresh, failure, and revocation paths as applicable. Keep sensitive placeholders short and route renewal loops outside consumer labels.\n\nCheck: No real credential material appears anywhere in the drawing. Bootstrap trust is explained rather than shifted into an unnamed secret. Access scope and retention behavior are stated from evidence. Authority boundaries and renewal arrows do not cross text.\n\nPitfalls: Calling a copied long-lived file dynamic secret management. Omitting the behavior when retrieval or renewal fails.\n\nExample: A workload authenticates through an agent, receives a scoped database credential, and renews its lease under a defined policy.\n\nAlternatives: Authorization graph, Data protection map."
